  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each sequence in turn. We need to calculate the limit of each sequence as $n$ approaches at infinity.\n\n- In sequence I, the dominant term in the denominator is ${n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n-1}{{n}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\u2061\\frac{(\\frac{n}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\u2061\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence II, the limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nThe limit exists, so the sequence converges.\n\n- In sequence III, the dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}+3}{3{n}^{2}-n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+\\frac{3}{{n}^{2}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, sequence III diverges.",
